\cf2 Task 5.4: Write unit tests for Article Fetcher - COMPLETED\
The unit tests I added cover all the requirements specified in the task:\
\
1. Test exponential backoff timing (Requirement 2.5)\
should use exponential backoff timing (1s, 2s, 4s): Verifies that retry attempts use exponential backoff with delays of 1000ms, 2000ms\
should not apply backoff delay on first attempt: Ensures no delay on the initial attempt\
should apply correct backoff delay on second attempt only: Tests that only one backoff delay is applied when the operation succeeds on the second try\
2. Test rate limit header parsing (Requirements 8.1, 8.2)\
should parse Retry-After header in seconds: Tests parsing of valid Retry-After headers and conversion to milliseconds\
should return 0 for missing Retry-After header: Handles missing headers gracefully\
should return 0 for invalid Retry-After header: Handles malformed header values\
should return 0 for non-axios errors: Handles non-HTTP errors properly\
should handle large Retry-After values: Tests with large timeout values\
3. Test request distribution over time (Requirements 8.1, 8.2)\
should distribute requests according to rate limit: Verifies that rate limiting is applied and requests complete successfully\
should allow burst requests within rate limit capacity: Tests that high-capacity rate limits allow quick successive requests\
should enforce rate limit when capacity is exceeded: Ensures rate limiting works when the limit is exceeded\
Key Features Tested:\
Exponential backoff: 1s, 2s delays as specified in requirements\
Rate limit compliance: Proper handling of rate limits and Retry-After headers\
Request distribution: Ensuring requests are properly spaced according to rate limits\
Error handling: Comprehensive testing of various error scenarios\
Header parsing: Correct parsing and handling of HTTP rate limit headers\
All tests are passing and validate the core functionality required by the specifications. The implementation follows the requirements from the design document and ensures the Article Fetcher behaves correctly under various conditions including network errors, rate limiting, and retry scenarios.\
